<time datetime="2023-02-13">February 13, 2023</time>
<article id="Public_IP" class="WebBits">
    <h2>TRY: Looking up your IP Address</h2>
    <p>
        Data runs the internet, and data always originates from somewhere. Two
        <abbr title="Internet Protocol">IP</abbr> addresses are immediately
        important.
    </p>
    <hr>
    <details>
        <summary>
            <strong>1.&rpar; Local IP: </strong>the <em>device</em> address
            assigned by a router or
            <abbr title="Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol">DHCP</abbr> server
            on the network.
        </summary>
        <p>
            The local ip can be found using
            <abbr title="PowerShell">PS</abbr> and
            <abbr title="Command Prompt">CMD</abbr>, as such:
        </p>
        <div id="TwoFigures">
            <figure class="twoFigures_PS PSFigure">
                <figcaption>PowerShell</figcaption>
                <code class="PS">
                    Get-NetIPAddress | ? {$_.AddressFamily -match "IPv4"} | select
                    IPAddress
                </code>
            </figure>
            <figure class="twoFigures_CMD CMDFigure">
                <figcaption>CMD</figcaption>
                <code class="CMD">ipconfig | findstr "IPv4"</code>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </details>
    <hr>
    <details>
        <summary>
            <strong>2.&rpar; Public IP: </strong> the <em>network</em> address
            assigned by an <abbr title="Internet Service Provider">ISP</abbr>.
        </summary>
        <p>
            A browser requires a connection into your network, so it's a good
            place to find your public visible ip address.
        </p>
        <aside class="examplePink">
            <h4>Find your public ip address using the browser:</h4>
            <ol id="BOINC-links">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.bing.com/search?q=what+is+my+ip+address" title="Bing: what is my IP address"
                       target="_blank" rel="external noopener">Search Engine</a>
                    - The browser can return the public ip
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.ipchicken.com/" title="IP Chicken" target="_blank" rel="external noopener">iP ChiCKEN</a>
                    - Websites can return and show the public ip
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.ip2location.com/demo/" title="IP2Location" target="_blank" rel="external noopener">
                        Location
                        Lookup
                    </a>
                    - The IP2Location website has location information along with
                    the public ip
                </li>
            </ol>
        </aside>
        <hr>
    </details>
    <aside class="exampleBlue">
        <p>
            <strong>Do NOT share your <u>public ip.</u></strong><br>It belongs to you and is secure not to share it with
            anyone.
        </p>
    </aside>
</article>
<acronyms-list></acronyms-list>
